1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,900
The record says: on hand, 55 bags, no reorder needed. What actually happened: the shelf has been empty since Wednesday.

2
00:00:03,900 --> 00:00:09,300
A supermarket in Bengaluru, June 2026. A synthetic world, calibrated against studies of real stores.

3
00:00:09,300 --> 00:00:15,700
Thursday, 7:40 pm. The 5 kg atta shelf has been empty since Wednesday afternoon. The handheld says 55.

4
00:00:15,700 --> 00:00:22,100
Every night the reorder system sees 55 bags, above its reorder point of 40. It orders nothing.

5
00:00:22,100 --> 00:00:28,000
The demand planner sees sales fall 59% in a week, and trims the forecast.

6
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:33,400
The category manager sees 5 kg’s share of atta fall from 40% to 25%.

7
00:00:33,400 --> 00:00:40,300
What actually happened: 56 bags were put away behind the rice. The books were right that the stock existed. Nobody could find it.

8
00:00:40,300 --> 00:00:46,700
39 open hours with an empty shelf. 71 bags customers wanted and could not buy.

9
00:00:46,700 --> 00:00:53,100
Availability that month: 97%, 75%, 77% or 78%. It depends who you ask.

10
00:00:53,100 --> 00:00:59,500
Scan every case into a backroom bin, and June’s lost bags fall from 157 to 1.

11
00:00:59,500 --> 00:01:04,500
Every system keeps a record. Domain knowledge is knowing when the record is wrong.
